{
  "description": "Vectors for the link message (changelog 54; the authority rules and display[] are changelog 55). Spec: docs/protocol.md, \"Links: link\" and \"Versioning\". Frames of the link message itself are the link-* cases of messages.json; this file has the rules behind them.",
  "specVersion": 1,
  "howToRead": "urls[]: isLinkUrl(url) must equal valid. It is the openable-URL rule of urls.json applied to the string AS SENT, with no trimming (whitespace at either end makes it invalid), at most 8,192 bytes; a synthesize case builds prefix + fill x fillCount + suffix and its byte size is in bytes. unchanged: true marks URLs a receiver must hand to the opener as exactly the same string. titles[] and sourceApps[]: cleanLinkText(input, 300 or 64 code points) must equal expected (null = absent). sendAs[]: after the hellos of one connection (the latest one counts; capabilities that are not an array count as none and non-string entries are skipped, and a hello with a bad capabilities is still accepted), does the sender put a link on the wire as a link or as a text? display[]: for a URL that is valid as a link, display is the address a receiver shows (docs/protocol.md, \"Showing the address of a link\": the host a browser connects to, lower case, IDNA punycode, IPv4 as dotted decimal, IPv6 compressed, the port only when it is not the scheme's default; for a host a browser refuses, the host as written with every character outside U+0021..U+007E as U+FFFD and no port) and warn says whether it carries a warning (the host as written is non-ASCII, the address has an xn-- label, it is an IP address, or the browser refuses it). Apart from the refused hosts, display equals new URL(url).host in a browser. textFallback[]: the text frame fields that stand in for a link when it goes as text (the id stays the same as the link's).",
  "maxUrlBytes": 8192,
  "maxTitleCodePoints": 300,
  "maxSourceAppCodePoints": 64,
